Reflected XSS into a Template Literal

Lab: PortSwigger Web Security Academy
Difficulty: Practitioner
Type: Reflected XSS

Vulnerability

The blog search functionality reflected user-controlled input inside a JavaScript template literal.

The application HTML-encoded <, >, single quotes, and double quotes, while backticks were escaped.

Despite these restrictions, the input remained executable within the JavaScript context.

Reconnaissance

The important observation was that the reflection was occurring inside a JavaScript template literal rather than directly inside HTML.

`user-controlled input`
    <script>
        var message = `0 search results for name`;
        document.getElementById('searchMessage').innerText = message;
    </script>

Because of this, HTML encoding alone was not sufficient to prevent JavaScript execution.

Exploitation

The approach was to break out of the template literal's string context and inject JavaScript.

${alert(1)};

    <script>
        var message = `0 search results for '${alert(1)}'`;
        document.getElementById('searchMessage').innerText = message;
    </script>


This allowed the injected code to execute within the existing JavaScript context.

Result

The injected JavaScript successfully executed alert() inside the template literal, solving the lab.

Root Cause

User-controlled input was inserted into a JavaScript template literal without being safely handled for the JavaScript context.

The application relied on HTML encoding, which does not provide adequate protection when untrusted data is placed inside JavaScript.

Key Takeaway

XSS defenses must account for the context in which the input is interpreted.

HTML encoding can protect against certain HTML injection scenarios, but it does not make arbitrary data safe when it is inserted into JavaScript.